{
  "id": "trennbare-verben",
  "name": "Phrasal verbs: the basics",
  "level": "A2",
  "status": "entwurf",
  "subtitle": "Learn each phrasal verb as one unit: get up, turn on, look for. Some can be split by a noun: turn the light on. With a pronoun you must split them: turn it on, not turn on it.",
  "briefMarks": [
    "phrasal verb",
    "turn the light on",
    "turn it on"
  ],
  "source": "icanspeak English original English text (grammar-a2 run, 2026-10-29)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      {
        "text": "verb",
        "variant": "verb"
      },
      {
        "text": "+ small word (up, on, off, down …)",
        "variant": "prep"
      },
      {
        "text": "= one new meaning"
      }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "I <span class=\"vp-verb\">get</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">up</span> at seven. · Please <span class=\"vp-verb\">turn</span> the light <span class=\"vp-prep\">on</span>. · <span class=\"vp-verb\">Turn</span> it <span class=\"vp-prep\">on</span>.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "A <strong>phrasal verb</strong> is a verb + a small word (<em>up, down, on, off, out, back</em>). The meaning is often new: <em>give up</em> = stop trying, <em>look for</em> = search. Learn the two words together, like one word.",
        "tag": "Verb + small word"
      },
      {
        "html": "Many phrasal verbs have <strong>no object</strong>: <em>I get up at seven. Please sit down. The baby woke up. Come back soon.</em> There is nothing to split.",
        "tag": "No object"
      },
      {
        "html": "With an object, a <strong>noun</strong> can go after the small word <em>or</em> in the middle: <em>Turn on the light.</em> = <em>Turn the light on.</em> <em>Put on your coat.</em> = <em>Put your coat on.</em> Both are fine.",
        "tag": "Noun: two places"
      },
      {
        "html": "A <strong>pronoun</strong> (<em>it, them, me, him, her</em>) always goes in the <strong>middle</strong>: <em>Turn it on. Put them on. Pick it up.</em> Never <s>turn on it</s>.",
        "tag": "Pronoun: in the middle"
      },
      {
        "html": "A few phrasal verbs <strong>cannot be split</strong>: <em>look for, look after</em>. The object always comes after: <em>I'm looking for my keys. → I'm looking for them.</em> If you are not sure, check a dictionary.",
        "tag": "look for"
      },
      {
        "html": "Typical mistakes: ✗ <em>Please turn on it.</em> → ✓ <em>Please turn it on.</em> · ✗ <em>I'm looking my keys.</em> → ✓ <em>I'm looking for my keys.</em> · ✗ <em>Take on your shoes.</em> → ✓ <em>Take off your shoes.</em> (a different small word is a different meaning)",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "Phrasal verbs are very common in everyday speech. Many commands use them: <em>Sit down. Turn it off. Hurry up.</em> See <a href=\"/imperatives\">Imperatives</a>. Phrasal verbs work like other verbs in the <a href=\"/present-simple\">present simple</a>: <em>she gets up, he turns it on</em>. You will meet many more at B1 and B2.",
    "reference": {
      "showLabel": "Show overview ▼",
      "hideLabel": "Hide overview ▲",
